ion/id #uuid "09a1af5e-aa76-5d08-a0f2-bbb3d534250b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4895"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "985d535d-1f51-578d-bb43-a7940cb01fa6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482603}] :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Radical Expression"} :step/id #uuid "4296398b-7885-5b41-b8e0-2c15cbbf70d6" :step/math-academy-id 17735 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078453 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078320 :question/id #uuid "dc345132-1bb6-5773-804c-9e0fab544c85" :question/math-academy-id "e-4905"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "698ccc70-a18b-5b7d-92e2-a87896649ef5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050436}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482541}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Radical Expressions"} :step/id #uuid "2cfaac75-e3ff-5746-a31f-a771de747f18" :step/math-academy-id 17875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079341 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079665 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n-3\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe first thing to realize is that there is an invisible multiplication symbol here. The above expression is short for\n\n$$\n-3\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}\\,\\sqrt{\\left(2^4-1\\right) \\div 3}\\,.\n$$\n\nWhen multiplying a radical by a number, we usually omit the additional multiplication symbol.\n\nNow, to simplify the given expression, we use PEMDAS.  In terms of the order of operations:\n\n- Expressions underneath radicals are treated as if they are contained within parentheses (the **P** in **P**EMDAS), so we always deal with this first.\n\n- Radicals themselves are treated as exponents (the **E** in P**E**MDAS).\n\nTherefore, following PEMDAS, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\sqrt{({2}^{4}-1)÷3} & = \\\\ -3\\sqrt{(16-1)÷3} & = \\\\ -3\\sqrt{15÷3} & = \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's our answer! \n\nTo get a numerical approximation, we need to use a calculator. In this case, we get\n\n$$\n-3 \\sqrt{5} = -3\\times \\sqrt{5} \\approx -6.708\\,204\n$$\n\nrounded to six decimal places." :tutorial/id #uuid "5eb86849-2816-5e44-b2e4-786b170cf6d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3274 :tutorial/title "Expressions Resulting in Radicals"} :step/id #uuid "9382a3ac-b4d0-5a78-a05e-5300d695a1ab" :step/math-academy-id 17998 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079667 :tutorial/content "Suppose we need to evaluate an expression like\n\n$$\n1+\\sqrt{4},\n$$\n\nwhere we have a square root sign. What would be the order of operations? It turns out that the root signs ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$ or $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{x}}$) can be thought of as parentheses. \nIn other words, we find the value under the square root first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\sqrt{4} & = \\\\ 1+2 & = \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSimilarly, let's evaluate $\\big(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27}\\big) \\div 2$.\n\nTo evaluate expressions with radicals, we use PEMDAS as before, but with the following additional rules:\n\n- expressions underneath radicals are treated the same as parentheses\n\n- radicals themselves are treated as exponents\n\nFollowing the order of operations, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-\\sqrt[3]{-27})÷2 & = \\\\ (1-\\sqrt[3]{(-3)^{3}})÷2 & = \\\\ (1-(-3))÷2 & = \\\\ 4÷2 & = \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3d4a0de2-cecd-5c77-8bf2-40997a53ac9a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3292 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b60636a5-2d22-51b2-971a-7edd2831849f" :step/math-academy-id 18074 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079332}}] :activity/title "Evaluating Numerical Expressions Containing Radicals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078630}]}
